// capability_table.def — THE list of optional OCR capabilities. No include
// guard: this is an X-macro database, included repeatedly with different X.
//
// ADDING A CAPABILITY IS ONE LINE HERE. That line then drives, generically:
//   * the HTTP query parameter name          (?<name>=1)
//   * the HTTP JSON body key                 {"<name>": true}
//   * the /capabilities features.<name> key
//   * the gRPC request field name            (must match, see proto_capability_bridge)
//   * the Python Pipeline.capabilities key
//   * per-backend availability               (backend::StageAvailability)
//
// The name is deliberately ONE string used for all of them. That convention is
// what lets gRPC be served by proto reflection instead of hand-written glue,
// and it is why a capability cannot be "added to the parser but forgotten in
// /capabilities" — there is only one place to add it.
//
// WHY AN X-MACRO rather than a struct of bools (what this replaces): a struct
// of bools cannot be iterated, so every consumer hand-wrote its own list and
// the lists drifted. Concretely, before this existed: `layout/tables/formulas`
// were threaded as three positional bools into six registrar signatures — and
// the gRPC one took them in a DIFFERENT ORDER (layout, readiness, table,
// formula), so transposing two of them compiled cleanly and silently disabled
// a feature. Nothing caught it.
//
// Rejected alternatives (see docs): enum + a parallel hand-maintained name
// array (the two lists can still desync in length/order — here both are
// emitted from the SAME source line); a runtime self-registering table like
// backend::BackendRegistrar (right for vendors, which are independent TUs
// nothing can enumerate centrally; wrong for a small closed set every
// transport must agree on — a registry you can forget to consult reproduces
// the silent-drop bug instead of preventing it); external codegen (overkill at
// this size).
//
// X(Enumerator, wire_name, implied_mask, error_code, remediation_hint)
//
//   implied_mask: capabilities this one REQUIRES. Tables and formulas are
//   per-layout-region stages, so requesting either implicitly needs layout —
//   encoding it here means no endpoint has to remember that rule.
//
//   error_code + remediation_hint: what a client gets when it asks for this
//   capability on a server that did not load it. These live HERE, next to the
//   capability, because the alternative — each endpoint writing its own
//   rejection — is how two endpoints end up returning different codes for the
//   same condition. The codes are the ones docs/reference/http.md already documents,
//   so moving them here changes no wire contract.
//
//   The hint says what an OPERATOR must do (the client cannot fix a missing
//   model), which is why it names env vars and model paths rather than request
//   parameters.

#ifndef TURBO_CAPABILITY_IMPLIES
#define TURBO_CAPABILITY_IMPLIES(cap) (1u << static_cast<unsigned>(::turbo_ocr::capability::CapabilityId::cap))
#endif

// clang-format off
#define TURBO_CAPABILITY_TABLE(X)                                              \
  X(Layout,         "layout",     0u,                                          \
    "LAYOUT_DISABLED",                                                         \
    "the layout model is not loaded: either models/layout/layout.onnx is "      \
    "missing, or the server was started with DISABLE_LAYOUT=1")                \
  X(Table,          "tables",     TURBO_CAPABILITY_IMPLIES(Layout),            \
    "TABLE_BACKEND_DISABLED",                                                  \
    "no table backend is configured: start the server with TABLE_BACKEND= + "  \
    "TABLE_SLANEXT_ENCODER_ONNX=")                                             \
  X(Formula,        "formulas",   TURBO_CAPABILITY_IMPLIES(Layout),            \
    "FORMULA_BACKEND_DISABLED",                                                \
    "no formula backend is configured: start the server with FORMULA_ONNX= + " \
    "FORMULA_TOKENIZER=")                                                      \
  X(DocOrientation, "autorotate", 0u,                                          \
    "AUTOROTATE_DISABLED",                                                     \
    "the doc-orientation model is not loaded: set DOC_ORI_ONNX= to a "        \
    "PP-LCNet_x1_0_doc_ori model (default models/doc_ori.onnx)")
// clang-format on
